Subject: [PATCH] dynarec: a registry of x86 functions that run as native code; Storm's explode (Project Diablo 2, WowBox64)

Patch 0012 runs D2Lang.dll's string-key lookup as native code from a hook at the start of a block.
This patch makes that hook a registry (native_x86.c): each known function has its own check of its
code bytes and its own native code, and the block calls the one whose code it starts at. When the
native code did the work, the block does the function's `ret` as Box64 translates it (with CALLRET a
direct return into the caller's code), not through the epilog and a lookup of the return address.
When the native code refuses a call, the block runs the x86 code as before.
BOX64_DYNAREC_NATIVEX86=0 (was BOX64_DYNAREC_D2LANG) turns them all off. Box64's log says once for
each when it is used.

The second function is Storm.dll's PKWARE decompressor ("explode"), which unpacks the game's data
files: at a game entry it is about 190 ms of a game thread on a Snapdragon 662, and the game's main
thread waits for that thread. storm_explode.c does what it does on the caller's own work area, with
the same layout (its tables, its window, its input buffer, its bit state), and does what Storm's two
memory callbacks do: the same result, registers, flags, work area, parameter block and output. It
takes a call only when the callbacks are one of Storm's two pairs of memory callbacks (those of its
decompress function and those of its MPQ sector reader, which unpacks most of the data), and refuses (the x86 code runs) a header that it
does not take (a first read of 4 bytes or less, a dictionary size other than 4 to 6 bits, a type
other than binary or ASCII) and memory that overlaps. One call unpacks a whole block, so the check
that the code is unchanged compares all of it at each call (64-bit words).

Tested against Storm's x86 explode itself on an x86 PC (the DLL's sections at its base): blocks of
six kinds, packed by Storm's own x86 implode (binary and ASCII, dictionaries of 1, 2 and 4 KB),
some corrupted or cut short, unpacked into outputs of the right size, too small or larger, with
either pair of callbacks: 3204 calls (2471 to the end, 529 that end on bad data), no difference in eax, ecx, edx, the kept
registers, esp, the six flags, the work area (all 0x3134 bytes), the parameter block, the output
with its margins, and the stack from the return address up; 200 of 200 round trips give the data
back; the 4 refusal cases are refused with nothing changed. Ten planted errors: eight make the test
fail; two change nothing that can be seen (a shift mask past the largest shift, a count below the
stack pointer).
---
